---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import DocPage from '../../components/docs/DocPage.astro';
import ApiTable from '../../components/docs/ApiTable.astro';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import { rustDark, rustLight } from '../../../shiki-rust-themes.mjs';
import { SRC_URL, MULTIVECTOR_URL, METRIC_URL } from '../../consts';

const SHIKI = { light: rustLight, dark: rustDark };

const title = 'Gate kernels — DeepCausality Quantum';
const description =
  'Five kernels over the Clifford ket, each with a PropagatingEffect wrapper that routes failure to the error channel; the signature-dependent Dirac adjoint; and the six Haruna gauge-field logical gates.';

const pairs = `// The kernel returns a Result. Use it from ordinary Rust.
let p: FloatType = born_probability_kernel(&state, &basis)?;

// The wrapper returns a PropagatingEffect. Use it from a causaloid.
let effect: PropagatingEffect<FloatType> = born_probability(&state, &basis);`;
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/gates/">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" />

  <DocPage
    eyebrow="Layer · Gates"
    title="Gate kernels on the causal monad"
    lede="Quantum mechanics as ordinary Rust functions, each paired with a lift into the effect propagation the rest of DeepCausality speaks."
    sourceHref={`${SRC_URL}/types/qgates`}
    sourceLabel="src/types/qgates"
  >
    <h2>The carrier</h2>

    <p>
      A state here is a <code>HilbertState&lt;R&gt;</code>, which is a
      minimal-left-ideal element of a Clifford algebra. It lives in
      <a href={MULTIVECTOR_URL}>deep_causality_multivector</a> rather than in
      this crate, and this crate adds two aliases for it,
      <code>Operator&lt;R&gt;</code> and <code>Gate&lt;R&gt;</code>. Both name
      the same type; the two names exist because a reader of a signature wants
      to know which role the argument plays.
    </p>

    <p>
      Metric signatures come from
      <a href={METRIC_URL}>deep_causality_metric</a>, the workspace's single
      source of truth. This crate defines no metric type of its own, which is
      why a mismatch between two operands is reported as
      <code>MetricMismatch</code> rather than resolved by a conversion nobody
      asked for.
    </p>

    <h2>Kernel and wrapper</h2>

    <p>
      Five kernels carry the mechanics: <code>born_probability_kernel</code>
      computes <code>|⟨basis|state⟩|²</code>,
      <code>expectation_value_kernel</code> computes <code>⟨ψ|A|ψ⟩</code> for a
      Hermitian <code>A</code>, <code>apply_gate_kernel</code> computes
      <code>U|ψ⟩</code>, <code>commutator_kernel</code> computes
      <code>[A, B]</code>, and <code>fidelity_kernel</code> compares an intended
      state against a realized one.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each has a wrapper of the same name without the suffix. The kernel returns
      a <code>Result</code>; the wrapper returns a
      <code>PropagatingEffect</code> and routes a failure onto the error channel
      of the arity-5 causal monad. Neither form is preferred. A causaloid wants
      the wrapper, and ordinary Rust wants the kernel.
    </p>

    <Code code={pairs} lang="rust" themes={SHIKI} />

    <p>
      The Born kernel does more than square an amplitude. It rejects a metric
      mismatch up front, rejects a non-finite result, and rejects a probability
      outside a narrow band around <code>[0, 1]</code> before clamping the
      survivor into range. A value that drifts out of bounds is a symptom of a
      malformed state, and reporting it is more useful than clamping it away.
    </p>

    <h2>Which adjoint is the right one</h2>

    <p>
      The inner product on a Clifford ket depends on the metric signature, and
      getting this wrong does not produce an error; it produces zero. On a
      positive, Euclidean signature the Dirac adjoint is reversion together with
      coefficient conjugation, which is <code>QuantumOps::dag</code>. On a
      negative <code>Cl(0,n)</code> signature that form is degenerate: it is
      identically zero on the minimal left ideal, so a correctly normalized ket
      would report a Born probability of 0.
    </p>

    <p>
      The metric-correct adjoint there is the Clifford conjugation, grade
      involution composed with reversion, again with coefficient conjugation.
      The grade-<code>k</code> blade picks up the sign
      <code>(−1)^(k(k+1)/2)</code>. <code>clifford_conjugation</code> exposes it,
      and <code>dirac_bracket_kernel</code> gives the inner product built on it.
    </p>

    <p>
      The choice is made by inspecting the metric rather than by a flag. A
      uniformly positive signature takes <code>dag</code>, a uniformly negative
      one takes the Clifford conjugation, and a mixed signature is refused with
      <code>UnsupportedMetric</code>. Refusing the mixed case is deliberate: no
      single involution is correct there, so returning something would be worse
      than returning nothing.
    </p>

    <h2>The Haruna logical gates</h2>

    <p>
      Six gates come from Haruna's gauge field formalism (arXiv:2511.15224):
      <code>logical_s</code>, <code>logical_z</code>, <code>logical_x</code>,
      <code>logical_hadamard</code>, <code>logical_cz</code> and
      <code>logical_t</code>. Each is an exponential of a polynomial function of
      the electric and magnetic gauge fields, so each needs a matrix exponential
      over the multivector algebra.
    </p>

    <p>
      That exponential is a Taylor series with a budget, and the way it fails is
      the interesting part. <code>exp(0)</code> returns the identity immediately,
      which is a genuine result. An exponent whose norm is non-finite or exceeds
      1e6 is rejected before the loop starts. A term that overflows during
      accumulation is rejected. And a series that exhausts its 64-term budget
      without reaching a tolerance of 1e-12 is rejected as
      <code>CalculationError</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Returning the truncated sum would have been easier. It would also be wrong
      by many orders of magnitude for an exponent norm above roughly 16. Falling
      back to the identity would be worse still, because a real identity gate
      and a silent failure would then be indistinguishable, which is exactly the
      ambiguity a caller must not face.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each of the six has a <code>PropagatingEffect</code> wrapper in the same
      pattern as the mechanics kernels: <code>haruna_s_gate</code> and its five
      siblings. The wrappers take a real multivector field and lift it into the
      complex algebra; the <code>logical_*</code> functions underneath take the
      complex field directly.
    </p>

    <h2>The two traits</h2>

    <p>
      <code>QuantumGates</code> supplies the standard constructors:
      <code>gate_identity</code>, <code>gate_x</code>, <code>gate_y</code>,
      <code>gate_z</code>, <code>gate_hadamard</code> and
      <code>gate_cnot</code>. <code>QuantumOps&lt;R&gt;</code> supplies the
      Dirac-notation operations on a state: <code>dag</code>,
      <code>bracket</code>, <code>expectation_value</code> and
      <code>normalize</code>.
    </p>
  </DocPage>

  <div class="wide-wrap">
    <ApiTable layer="gates" title="The gate surface" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wide-wrap {
    max-width: var(--w-panel);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 var(--space-4) var(--space-9);
  }
  @media (min-width: 720px) { .wide-wrap { padding: 0 var(--space-5) var(--space-9); } }
</style>
